            <div xml:id="d183">

                <pb facs="0261" n="153" sameAs="frus:frus1893/pg153"/>

                <head><hi rendition="#italic">Mr. Young to Mr. Gresham.</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Legation of the United States,<lb/>Guatemala and
                                Honduras</hi>.<lb/><hi rendition="#italic"
                            >Guatemala</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1893-10-19"><hi rendition="#italic">October 19, 1893</hi></date>.
                        (Received November 2.)</dateline>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 32.]</seg></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: Since my last, announcing the assumption by
                    the President of the supreme control of the Government, no new developments have
                    occurred. The President is inaugurating some very salutary measures. He has
                    removed all duties from all live stock imported into the Republic, also all
                    duties on salted meats, which will render that article much cheaper than it has
                    been, and it will be of interest to meat-packers in the United States. The
                    Government is working well, and the Republic is enjoying a season of profound
                    peace. It is feared that there is not perfect tranquility in Honduras. It is
                    very difficult to get reliable news from that Republic, the telegraph wires
                    being down. I will be in condition to report more fully in my next.</p>
                <p>I am, etc.,</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">P. M. B. Young.</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
